#heading(level: 1, numbering: none, outlined: true)[ABSTRACT]

Web application testing at the Department of Information Technology, Politeknik Negeri Malang, faces challenges in ensuring the quality of academic applications such as Kompen, Jayanti, and Whistleblower. Conventional server-uptime monitoring cannot guarantee that an application's core functions -- login, role-based access control (RBAC), input validation, and CRUD operations -- actually work correctly, while automation testing tools such as Selenium, Cypress, and Playwright still require manual test case authoring, which becomes a bottleneck. TathyaTest is presented as an automatic test case generator that crawls the rendered DOM using Playwright, builds an element model and a per-role access matrix, and then generates and executes functional test cases automatically. This research measures the effectiveness of TathyaTest in generating automatic test cases, improving functional test coverage, and validating application functional expectations. The Prototyping method is applied iteratively from a simple CRUD prototype to Playwright integration for cross-browser testing (Chrome, Firefox, WebKit), evaluated on three case-study subjects (a Laravel Blade application, a Laravel Inertia-React application, and SauceDemo as an external baseline).

Evaluation results show 95.5% element-model coverage on Blade and 100.0% on Inertia-React, with SUT-level route coverage of 100.0% on Blade and 87.5% on Inertia-React, and perfect cross-browser agreement (Fleiss' kappa κ = 1.00) on all three subjects. Fault-detection accuracy, measured through controlled injection of 14 detectable faults, reached 100.0% on Blade and 85.7% on Inertia-React -- both meeting the 85% target and far exceeding the manual baseline (21.4%) -- with every negative-control fault surviving undetected on both subjects. TathyaTest generated 104 test cases for Blade, 106 for Inertia-React, and 31 for SauceDemo, with an estimated 30.0--32.7× time saving compared to manual test authoring (Mann-Whitney U, p < 0.001) and a lower brittle-locator ratio than the public comparison baseline suites. Testing also exposed two real defects in the case-study applications, confirming that the generated test cases can find genuine functional problems.

The outputs of this research include a TathyaTest prototype at TRL 4 and a manuscript prepared for the Jurnal Teknologi Informasi dan Ilmu Komputer (JUTI). This research contributes to Software Testing and Quality Assurance through an automated test case generation method based on rendered-DOM crawling, forming the foundation of a five-year roadmap for TathyaTest's development with AI and machine learning integration.

